Brute-forcing in the context of computer security refers to a trial-and-error method used to decode encrypted data such as passwords or to find a key by systematically checking all possible combinations. In JavaScript, you can implement a simple brute-force algorithm, but it's important to note that brute-forcing is generally considered unethical and illegal when used without proper authorization. It's often used in penetration testing with permission, but even then, it should be done responsibly and within legal boundaries.

Here's a very basic example of a brute-force algorithm in JavaScript that attempts to guess a 4-digit PIN. This is for educational purposes only and should not be used for any unauthorized activities.

```javascript
function bruteForcePin(pin) {
  const attempts = 0;
  const maxAttempts = 10000; // Set a limit to prevent infinite loops
  const possibleDigits = '0123456789';

  for (let i = 0; i < possibleDigits.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < possibleDigits.length; j++) {
      for (let k = 0; k < possibleDigits.length; k++) {
        for (let l = 0; l < possibleDigits.length; l++) {
          attempts++;
          const guess = possibleDigits[i] + possibleDigits[j] + possibleDigits[k] + possibleDigits[l];
          if (guess === pin) {
            console.log(`PIN found: ${guess} after ${attempts} attempts.`);
            return;
          }
          if (attempts >= maxAttempts) {
            console.log('Max attempts reached. Aborting brute-force.');
            return;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  console.log('PIN not found.');
}

// Example usage:
const targetPin = '1234'; // The PIN you are trying to brute-force
bruteForcePin(targetPin);
```

This script will attempt every possible combination of 4 digits until it either finds the correct PIN or reaches the maximum number of attempts. Keep in mind that this is a very inefficient method and would take an impractical amount of time to find a 4-digit PIN, as there are 10,000 possible combinations (from 0000 to 9999).

In real-world scenarios, brute-forcing is not a practical method for cracking passwords due to the time and computational power required. Modern systems use strong encryption and other security measures to protect against such attacks. Always use ethical hacking practices and have proper authorization when testing security systems.

